I've completed a thorough review of the app launcher, the UI store, the navigation module, the router, and every app component. Below is my analysis, the unified design, and the concrete implementation.

---

## 1. Analysis & Pre-Thinking

**Core requirement:** Give *every* app a mobile route, and collapse the many ad‑hoc “open” paths into **one** decision point (“panel on desktop / route on mobile”).

**What the codebase actually does today (verified):**

- **Two divergent open strategies coexist:**
  - `store/ui.js` exposes many `openX()` actions that call `showApp()` (a **panel-only** primitive) with **no mobile fallback** → they silently do nothing on mobile: `openVibeCoding`, `openProjects`, `openTeams`, `openWorkspaces`, `openTeamChannel`, `openTeamDM`, `openTeamMediaLibrary`, `openTutorial`, `openMediaLibrary`, `openFileInViewer`, `openChatLogs`, `toggleLogs`.
  - Only `openChat`, `openWorkspace`, `openMeet`, `openPlanning` branch on mode; `navigate.js` branches again in `apps.*`, `chats.*`, `meets.*`, `workspaces.*`, `browsers.*`.
  - The generic launcher `setActiveTab()` (used by `AppsSection`, `MainMenu`, `SettingsMenu`, `QuickBar`, `MobileMenu`…) **always** calls `showApp()` → does nothing on mobile.

- **Routes are inconsistent with the navigation module:** `navigate.js` pushes `/files`, `/wiki`, `/team`, `/views`, `/profiles`, `/knowledge` but **`router/index.ts` only defines** `/`, `/chats*`, `/meet*`, `/workspaces*`, `/browsers*`, `/kanban`, `/messenger`, `/desktop/*`, `/analytics`. Every other push falls through the catch‑all and redirects to `/`.

**App inventory vs. mobile route coverage (current):**

| appId (`appComponentsMap`) | Component | Mobile route today |
|---|---|---|
| `home` | CodxWelcomeView | ✅ `/` |
| `tasks` | KanbanBoardView | ✅ `/kanban` |
| `chat` | ChatView | ✅ `/chats/...` |
| `workspace` | AppWindow | ✅ `/workspaces/:id/:name` |
| `workspaces` | WorkspacesManager | ✅ `/workspaces` |
| `browser-sessions` | BrowserSessionList | ✅ `/browsers` |
| `messenger` | MessengerView | ✅ `/messenger` |
| `meet` | MeetView | ✅ `/meet` |
| `analytics` | MetricsDashboard | ✅ `/analytics` |
| `wiki, docs, knowledge, knowledge_settings, profiles, settings, global-settings, account, file-explorer, file-viewer, projects, activity, log-viewer, chat-logs, metrics, vibe-coding, team-channel, team-dm, team-media-library` | … | ❌ **no route** |

**Strategic approach:**

1. Introduce a single **app registry** (`config/apps.js`) that, per app, declares the panel component, the desktop key/params, and the **mobile route**. The registry replaces the scattered `isMobile ? … : …` checks.
2. Introduce **one** `openApp(appKey, payload)` (in `navigate.js`), exposed via `$storex.ui.openApp`. It picks panel vs route from a single predicate.
3. Add a generic **`AppRouteView`** host + all missing routes so every app is reachable by URL on mobile without 19 bespoke wrapper files.
4. Point every existing opener (store actions + `setActiveTab` on mobile) at `openApp`.

**Dependencies & risks:**

- **Mode predicate.** I use `$storex.ui.isMobile` (width ≤ 1024) — the same signal `showApp` and `chats.js` already use, and the only one that maps cleanly to “desktop = panel, mobile = route”. ⚠️ Flagged below because `openChat` *currently* uses `isDesktopMode` (route name). **This is the single semantic decision to confirm.**
- Deep-link payloads (a chat object, a team member) can't live in a URL; `openApp` stashes them in a transient map that `AppRouteView` reads (URL params are the fallback after a hard refresh).
- Desktop panel dedup keys are preserved (`chat-<id>`, `workspace-<key>`, …) to avoid duplicate panels on restored layouts.
- `$storex.$navigation` is referenced by `ui.js` but is **never assigned** in `router/index.ts` — added.

---

## 2. Minimum Changes Approach

1. **`src/config/apps.js`** *(new)* — registry + transient params hand-off.
2. **`src/views/AppRouteView.vue`** *(new)* — generic route host resolving the app from `route.meta.app`.
3. **`src/router/index.ts`** *(modify)* — add routes for every uncovered app; register `$storex.$navigation`.
4. **`src/router/navigate.js`** *(modify)* — add `openApp`; delegate `apps.*` to it.
5. **`src/store/ui.js`** *(modify)* — add `openApp` action; make `setActiveTab` route on mobile; delegate all `openX`/panel openers.

---

## 3. File Changes

### 1) New file — app registry

```js src/config/apps.js
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Single source of truth for "how to open an app".
//
// Each entry describes an app that can be opened either:
//   • as a desktop dockview PANEL  (expert view mode / desktop screens), or
//   • as a mobile ROUTE            (mobile screens / router mode).
//
// `openApp(appKey, payload)` (see router/navigate.js) reads this registry and
// picks the right strategy, so the rest of the app never needs to branch on
// isMobile / isDesktopMode again.
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Transient payload hand-off for mobile routes. Some apps need rich objects
// (a chat, a team member, a workspace…) that can't be encoded in the URL.
// `openApp` stashes them here and <AppRouteView> reads them back per app.
const PENDING = {}

export function setPendingParams(appId, params) {
  PENDING[appId] = params
}

export function getPendingParams(appId) {
  return PENDING[appId] || null
}

export function clearPendingParams(appId) {
  delete PENDING[appId]
}

export const APPS = {
  // ── Primary apps ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  home: {
    id: 'home', title: 'Home', component: 'home', icon: 'fa-solid fa-home',
    route: () => ({ name: 'home' })
